# bseries series definitions
#
# Declares what a series holds and how often a point is recorded, per key range.
# Without a definitions file every series in the database shares
# default_seconds_per_point and takes whatever point width the first write passed,
# which only works if the whole database stores one kind of measurement.
#
# Load with:
#
#     BSeries db;
#     db.data_directory = "/var/lib/bseries";
#     int loaded = db.loadDefinitions("/etc/bseries.conf");
#     if(loaded < 0) { /* malformed file, do not start */ }
#
# Format, one series family per line:
#
#     <name>  <keys>  <interval>  <type>  [null_fill]
#
#   name       label used in log messages only
#   keys       a single key, an inclusive N-M range, or * for everything
#   interval   seconds between points, optionally suffixed s, m or h
#   type       uint8 uint16 uint32 uint64
#              int8  int16  int32  int64
#              float32 float64
#              (aliases: byte, uchar, char, float, double)
#   null_fill  optional byte written for "no point recorded here",
#              as 0xNN or decimal. Defaults to the value for the type.
#
# Ranges are matched in file order and the first match wins, so declare narrow
# ranges before wide ones. Everything after a # is a comment.
#
# A definition applies when a series file is created. Once the file exists its own
# header is the authority, because the points already on disk were laid out to it.
# Changing a definition therefore does not retype existing series; the database
# warns when a definition and a file disagree and keeps using the file.


# name      keys            interval   type       null_fill

ping        1-9999          1s         uint8
temperature 10000-19999     60s        float32
power_draw  20000-29999     10s        uint32
door_state  30000-30999     5s         uint8      0x02


# On null fill
#
# Series are dense, so every gap has to be spelled out in the file and one value
# per type is reserved to mean "nothing was recorded". The defaults are:
#
#   float32 / float64   0xFF fill, which is a NaN. No real value is lost.
#   unsigned integers   0xFF fill, the maximum value. A uint8 series cannot
#                       store 255, a uint16 series cannot store 65535, and so on.
#   signed integers     0x80 fill, a large negative number. There is no clean
#                       sentinel for a signed type; pick one your data cannot
#                       produce and set it explicitly.
#
# The door_state line above is an example: the sensor reports 0 or 1, so 0x02 is
# free and makes a much better "no reading" marker than 255 would.
